  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Fri Oct 16 2026\n\nA rather chilly day is unfolding despite full sunshine. Clear\nskies, light winds, and a dry airmass will allow strong\nradiational cooling tonight, with below freezing temperatures \nexpected over most of the northeast plains again. Meanwhile, we'll\nsee a light downslope component develop along the Front Range\nkeeping most of Denver/Boulder areas a few degrees warmer. Most \nlocations northeast of Denver observed a hard killing freeze last \nnight (deemed as 28F or colder), so from this point forward we \nwill only be issuing highlights for those areas where a killing \nfreeze did not occur - namely the I-25 Corridor and far northeast \ncorner of the state. That means a Freeze Warning for Sedgwick and \nPhillips Counties tonight where lows are expected to dip into the\nmid to upper 20s. \n\nFor Saturday, an upper level ridge will build overhead with sunny\nskies and dry weather. Temperatures will be warming with highs\npushing into the upper 60s to mid 70s, a few degrees above normal.  \nOverall, another day of light winds can be expected with high \npressure in control. There will just a slight increase of \nsoutherly winds over the eastern plains, resulting in localized \nelevated fire weather conditions mainly over Lincoln and \nWashington Counties. \n\nOn Sunday, the upper level ridge axis will shift eastward with\nlight southwest flow aloft developing. This supports a continuation\nof above normal temperatures, with highs pushing into the 70s \nagain across most of the plains. Enough mid level moisture gets \ncaught up in this flow to warrant a low chance of showers in the \nmountains by Monday and Tuesday. That chance of showers may very \nwell increase Wednesday, depending on how much tropical moisture \npulls northward in advance of the next trough digging southward \noff the West Coast. \n\nWe are watching the arrival of this next storm system for the \nlatter portions of next week, when the West Coast trough pushes \neast across the Rockies. There is considerable uncertainty this \nfar out, especially as latest ensembles and operational runs are \ndiverging from recent runs, splitting off a lead shortwave across \nthe Central Rockies and Colorado Thursday or Thursday night, with \na lingering trough holding farther west through next weekend. With\nmore splitting being shown, the chances of significant\nprecipitation in our forecast area have decreased. The highest \nodds now favor southwest Colorado once again as many of the storms\nhave so far in September and October.",
